INT. GRAND CALDERON HOTEL - STALLED ELEVATOR - NIGHT

A mirrored brass cage, lights flickering. The floor indicator is frozen between 11 and 12. A tinny recording of a hotel jingle loops through a speaker.

HARLOW BENNETT, 40s, a rumpled suit, clutches a leather briefcase. PERPETUA ADEYEMI, 30s, a sharp black dress and silver heels, stares at her phone: no bars. RUSSELL KOVACS, 50s, a maintenance uniform, kneels at the control panel with a screwdriver.

HARLOW
How long is this going to take?

RUSSELL
Depends.

HARLOW
On what?

RUSSELL
On whether you want the truth.

PERPETUA
We want the truth.

RUSSELL
Then forty minutes. Maybe an hour.

HARLOW
An hour!

PERPETUA
Harlow, breathe.

HARLOW
Don't tell me to breathe, Perpetua, you know what's in this case.

PERPETUA
(sharp)
Quiet.

RUSSELL
What's in the case?

HARLOW
Paperwork.

RUSSELL
Paperwork that ticks?

Silence. Harlow's grip tightens. The light flickers. Perpetua glances at the briefcase, then at Harlow, then at the screwdriver in Russell's hand.

RUSSELL (CONT'D)
(gently)
It's been ticking since the lobby, friend.

PERPETUA
Oh, Harlow.

HARLOW
It's a watch.

RUSSELL
It's a bomb.

HARLOW
It's a watch!

The ticking is loud now. Regular. Quick.

PERPETUA
(very low)
Russell. Can you open the hatch?

RUSSELL
I can.

PERPETUA
Do it.
